# Show household urgency clearly

**Reference pattern.** These examples are simulated. They do not install Home Assistant screens or control a device.

Say what was observed, what to do next and how sure the information is. Do not make every maintenance record look like an emergency (WG-042, WG-046).

## Use three kinds of message

civica uses three kinds of household message: routine information, an event that needs attention, and unknown information. It does not copy clinical triage or its severity levels.

## Choose the message from evidence

A scheduled filter check is routine. Show the due date and say how the reminder was set. Do not use an emergency frame on a certificate, an inspection record or a service date.

<%= example('nhs-household-routine') %>

A current event can justify a more prominent message with a label. Name the device, say what it reports and when the update arrived. Give one next step. Do not infer a condition the device cannot measure.

<%= example('nhs-household-action') %>

Lost connection is different. It confirms neither a hazard nor an all-clear. Keep any unresolved event and say that no new observations are arriving (WG-033).

<%= example('nhs-household-unknown') %>

## Use colour as a second cue

Lead with words, such as “Check the freezer door” or “Sensor information is unavailable”. Use a neutral rule for routine information and the civica warning role for an event that needs attention. A theme colour change must not change that meaning. Do not invent a household colour code for severity.

## Accessibility and content

Give each message a heading, and give the content around it its own heading. Keep the observation and action short enough to read under pressure. Give the date, time and time zone when freshness matters. Do not rely on an icon alone to show severity. Announce a genuinely new important event once. Do not repeat a static message each time an entity value refreshes.

## Home Assistant boundary

A theme cannot decide whether something is physically safe. A production event needs a verified integration, a documented household rule, an owner and a route that exists. Keep native alarm behaviour, permissions and acknowledgements. Never offer a remote action that is unavailable. Never treat selecting “I have read this” as clearing a sensor.

Life-safety and emergency instructions need a separately reviewed product workflow and authoritative guidance. This pattern gives no medical advice, no emergency protocol and no guarantee of monitoring (WG-043). The examples are simulated interface states, not live warnings.
